{"id":"5b7d3ebb-7b2a-4c64-b719-357af965d1b8","object":"chat.completion","created":1784985962,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid case: nodes or required_symbols not list')\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    all_symbols = set()\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_dict[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n        all_symbols.add(node['symbol'])\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym!r} not found in nodes')\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid == 'root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols or not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        retained_symbols = {node_dict[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\\n        if all(sym in retained_symbols for sym in required_symbols):\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to interpret the task: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" for a parse tree, with the objective to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols and ensuring the \"interestingness\" is preserved (not defined but probably means that after removal, the tree still has certain properties; we might need to simulate a simple check). The description says \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we need to implement some reduction algorithm that, given nodes with removable flag, tries to remove them in some order, but only if after removal the required symbols are still present (i.e., the tree still contains nodes with those symbols) and maybe other constraints.\n\nGiven the fixtures, we see a payload with nodes: each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (utility might be used for ranking? but not mentioned). Also required_symbols list. There's also \"_case_nonce\" which is just an identifier.\n\nWe need to return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order. Probably we need to compute which nodes are removed and which are retained, respecting the constraints.\n\nThe description says: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should process nodes in descending depth order (deeper first). For nodes at same depth, use node ID as tie break (stable).\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So root cannot be removed. Also nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed. Also a node can only be removed if it has no children that are retained (i.e., all its children must be removed first? Or if any child is retained, the node cannot be removed? The phrase \"a node with retained children\" suggests if the node has any child that is still present (retained) then it cannot be removed. So removal must be bottom-up: you can only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose all descendants have been removed.\n\n\"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after removing a node, we need to check that the remaining nodes (including ancestors) cover all required symbols. Also maybe we need to ensure the tree is still \"grammar valid\"? Not specified what that means. Possibly just that the tree structure is maintained (parent-child relationships) and no orphaned nodes? But after removal, the children of a removed node would be lost; we must ensure that the required symbols might be present elsewhere. So we need to simulate removal, check if required symbols are still present (by traversing all retained nodes), and if yes, we can remove it.\n\nThe algorithm: \n- Build a tree structure from nodes (parent references). Determine leaf nodes.\n- Create a set of required symbols.\n- Create a set of initially retained nodes (all nodes).\n- We need to decide removal order. Process nodes in order: deeper nodes first (max depth), then by ID. For each node: if node is root, skip (cannot remove). If node symbol in required_symbols, skip. If node has any child that is retained (i.e., still in retained set), skip (because we cannot remove a node with retained children). Then tentatively remove: remove it from retained set temporarily, check if all required symbols are still present in retained set (i.e., there exists at least one retained node with that symbol). If yes, then confirm removal; record in removal order. If no, put it back (retain). Continue.\n- After processing all nodes, the retained set is final. Return sorted retained IDs (ascending?) and the removal order (list of IDs in order removed).\n\nThe requirement \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So output should be a dict with keys like \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\".\n\nAlso \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". What is infeasible? If required symbols are missing initially? Or if can't satisfy? Maybe we should validate that required_symbols are present in the initial tree; if not, raise ValueError. Also check that root exists and has proper parent relationships? The fixtures seem valid.\n\nAlso \"Do not mutate the input.\" So we operate on copies.\n\nThe \"utility\" field is present but not used in description; maybe it's for ranking? But the algorithm uses depth and ID for ordering, not utility. So ignore utility.\n\nNow, implement solve(case). The case payload is the inner object: nodes list and required_symbols list (and _case_nonce). We should ignore _case_nonce.\n\nWe need to write Python code that does the above. Ensure we use standard library only.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate: nodes must be a list; required_symbols is list; each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility; but we only need some. We can check that required_symbols are present in initial set of symbols from nodes. Raise ValueError if missing.\n2. Build a dict mapping id to node object (maybe as a simple dict) and also map parent to children.\n3. Initialize retained set with all node IDs.\n4. Sort nodes by depth descending, then by ID (stable, so sort by tuple (-depth, id) or (depth, id) reverse? We'll sort by depth descending, then id ascending. Since ID is string, we can compare directly.\n5. removal_order = []\n6. For each node in sorted order:\n   - if node['id'] == 'root': continue (since root is always not removable, but it might have removable flag false already). But we can also check removable flag? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So even if removable is True, we cannot remove root or required symbols. However, the payload has root with removable: false. So it's safe.\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue\n   - check if any child of this node is still in retained set. children list from parent mapping. If yes, continue.\n   - tentatively remove: remove id from retained set.\n   - check required symbols: for each req_sym in required_symbols, check if any retained node has that symbol. If all present, then confirm removal: append node['id'] to removal_order; keep removed from retained set. Else, add id back to retained set (undo).\n7. After loop, retained set contains final retained nodes.\n8. Sort retained IDs ascending (since IDs are alphanumeric, sort lexicographically? The examples: 'n1','n2','n3','n4','n5','n6','root'. Root is 'root', others like 'n1', so sorting lexicographically will put 'n1' before 'n2' etc. That's fine. Or maybe by depth order? The requirement says \"sorted retained IDs\" likely ascending string order. We'll do sorted(retained).\n9. Removal order is list in order removed.\n10. Return dict: {\"retained_ids\": list_of_ids, \"removal_order\": list_of_ids}\n\nBut wait: the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It doesn't specify the exact keys. We'll use \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". The example fixture output might expect something else? Not provided. We need to be consistent with typical format. Could be \"retained\" and \"removed\". But to be safe, we'll use those keys.\n\nAlso note: \"Preserve every source identifier needed to audit the decision, and compute aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\" This suggests we should return the actual step-by-step removal decisions. Our removal_order does that.\n\nOne nuance: The algorithm as described may not be optimal because after removing some nodes, other nodes that were previously children of removed nodes become leaves and can be considered again? Our loop processes nodes in a fixed order (depth descending). When we remove a node, its children (if any) are also removed implicitly? Wait: If we remove a node, what happens to its children? In a tree reduction, removing a node would typically also remove its subtree. But here, the node's children might still be retained if we didn't remove them yet? However, the rule \"a node with retained children\" prevents removal of a node if any child is retained. So we only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose children have already been removed. Our process goes deeper first, so leaves are removed first. When we remove a leaf, its parent might later become a \"leaf\" (if all its children removed). Then we can consider removing the parent. But our loop is fixed; after removing a leaf, we don't re-evaluate the parent in the same loop because we already processed the parent? Since parent has shallower depth, it will be processed later (because we sort depth descending). For depth = 2 (leaf), then depth = 1 (parents). So when we remove a leaf at depth 2, the parent at depth 1 will be processed after all depth 2 nodes. So the parent's children set may have been reduced (some removed). So we need to check children dynamically from the current retained set. That is fine.\n\nBut what about nodes that become removable only after multiple removals? Since we process depths in descending order, all deeper nodes are processed before shallower ones. So by the time we process a parent, all its children have been considered already (some might have been removed). So the condition \"no retained children\" will be correctly evaluated at that time. However, if a parent has children that were not removed (because they are required or non-removable), the parent cannot be removed. That's correct.\n\nWhat about a node that is at depth 1 but has a child that is at depth 2 but that child is non-removable (removable=false) or required symbol, so it remains. Then parent cannot be removed. So good.\n\nPotential issue: Our loop processes each node exactly once. After removing a node, we don't revisit its ancestors. But since we process ancestors later (shallower depth), that's fine. However, suppose there is a node at depth 1 with many children. Some children removed, some remain. The parent will be considered after all children processed; if it has remaining children, it cannot be removed. That's correct.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nNow, we need to output source code in JSON string. The response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being a Python source string. So we must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWe'll write the solve function with proper indentation. Use triple quotes inside? Better to write normal code and then escape. Since we are generating JSON, we need to escape newlines as \\n. We'll produce a string.\n\nAlternative: we can use json.dumps to encode the string, but we have to do it manually.\n\nLet me craft the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Validate\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case: nodes or required_symbols not list\")\n    # Build mappings\n    node_dict = {}\n    children = {}\n    all_symbols = set()\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_dict[nid] = node\n        children[nid] = []\n        all_symbols.add(node['symbol'])\n    # Check required symbols present\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' not found in nodes\")\n    # Build parent-child relations\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node.get('parent')\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\n    # Initially retain all nodes\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    # Order nodes: deeper first, then by id (string sort)\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        # cannot remove root\n        if nid == 'root':\n            continue\n        # cannot remove required symbol nodes\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # try remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        # check required symbols still present\n        retained_symbols = {node_dict[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\n        if all(sym in retained_symbols for sym in required_symbols):\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n        else:\n            # undo removal\n            retained.add(nid)\n    # Final retained list sorted\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n```\n\nPotential improvements: we should also check that root is present and parent relationships form a tree? Not needed for given fixtures.\n\nNow, we need to ensure we raise ValueError for invalid cases. Also, note that we ignored 'removable' field. The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It didn't say to check removable flag. However, the nodes have 'removable' boolean. But the algorithm says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and then \"Tentatively remove a node only if ...\". It doesn't explicitly say we must only consider nodes with removable=True. But common sense: reduction should only remove nodes marked removable. Looking at Perses, it removes nodes that are marked as deletable. In the fixtures, root has removable false, others have some true and false. So probably we should only consider nodes where removable is True. The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It does not say \"only remove removable nodes\", but it's implied by \"register parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". The \"removable\" flag likely indicates which nodes are candidates for removal. So we should add a check: if not node['removable']: continue. That would be consistent.\n\nAlso, the phrase \"grammar_valid\" might mean that the tree after removal must still conform to the grammar? But we have no grammar. Possibly the removable flag and required symbols capture that. So we'll add check for removable flag.\n\nThus update: if not node.get('removable', True): continue? Actually, nodes always have removable. We'll check `if not node['removable']: continue`.\n\nNow, final code.\n\nNow, we need to produce the JSON string. Escape it properly. We'll write the source as a string with triple quotes? But we have to escape newlines as \\n within JSON string. So we'll construct the Python code as a single line with \\n escapes, or we can use a raw string inside JSON. Better to write as multi-line in our response but then escape newlines? The requirement: \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we need to produce a valid JSON string where the value for \"implementation\" is a string containing valid Python code with \\n representing newlines. So we can write our Python code as a multi-line string and then replace newlines with \\n and backslashes with \\\\ and quotes with \\\". But easier: in our response, we'll write the Python code as a literal Python string containing newlines; then we can use json.dumps to produce the JSON. But since we are constructing manually, we'll do it step by step.\n\nLet me produce the Python code string with proper escaping.\n\nThe code above has newlines, indentation, quotes. We'll escape: double quotes inside the code (like 'node[\"id\"]') will become \\\"node[\\\"id\\\"]\\\" but actually within Python source, we use single quotes for keys, so no conflict. The only double quotes are in raise ValueError strings. Those will need to be escaped as \\\". Also backslashes: none. So we can produce.\n\nBut to be safe, I'll write the code without any double quotes; use single quotes for all strings. Our error message uses double quotes, change to single.\n\nAlso, we need to avoid using \"case\" variable name that conflicts? It's fine.\n\nLet me rewrite code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError('Invalid case: nodes or required_symbols not list')\n    node_dict = {}\n    children = {}\n    all_symbols = set()\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_dict[nid] = node\n        children[nid] = []\n        all_symbols.add(node['symbol'])\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym!r} not found in nodes')\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node.get('parent')\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid == 'root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols or not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        retained_symbols = {node_dict[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\n        if all(sym in retained_symbols for sym in required_symbols):\n            removal_order.append(nid)\n        else:\n            retained.add(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\n```\n\nNow we need to encode this as JSON string. We'll ensure: \n- newlines become \\n\n- quotes: we used single quotes, so inside JSON they are fine. But we have f-string with !r which is fine. No double quotes.\n- backslashes: none.\nThus we can just replace newline with \\n and tab with \\t? Actually indentation uses spaces, we keep spaces.\n\nLet me construct the string:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Invalid case: nodes or required_symbols not list')\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    all_symbols = set()\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_dict[nid] = node\\n        children[nid] = []\\n        all_symbols.add(node['symbol'])\\n    for sym in required_symbols:\\n        if sym not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym!r} not found in nodes')\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node.get('parent')\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(node['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid == 'root' or node['symbol'] in required_symbols or not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children[nid])\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        retained_symbols = {node_dict[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\\n        if all(sym in retained_symbols for sym in required_symbols):\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\"\n\nWe must escape backslashes (none) and quotes: The only potential quotes are inside the f-string: f'Required symbol {sym!r} not found in nodes' uses single quotes, fine. That's it.\n\nNow wrap in JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"...\"}\n\nMake sure no trailing spaces.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1922,"completion_tokens":4851,"total_tokens":6773,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1920},"completion_tokens_de